# YouTube Trending Search Tool

A local search dashboard for 133,000+ trending YouTube videos across 11 countries.

---

## Setup

1. Put all 4 files in the same folder:
   - `search_server.py`
   - `search_dashboard.html`
   - `ytdata.json`
   - `README.md`

2. Run the server:
   ```
   python3 search_server.py
   ```

3. Open your browser to: **http://localhost:8765**

> ⚠️ Always open `http://localhost:8765` in your browser — do not double-click the HTML file directly, as it won't be able to load data that way.

---

## On startup

The page automatically loads the **all-time top 50 most popular** and **top 50 most controversial** videos from the entire dataset — no search needed. You'll see these as soon as the page opens.

---

## Searching

The filter panel is on the **left sidebar**, styled with collapsible Include and Exclude sections. Results appear on the **right**.

### Include & Exclude

Each section has an **Any field** bar at the top, plus three expandable sub-filters:

| Sub-filter | Searches in |
|------------|-------------|
| Any field  | Title + tags + description (combined) |
| Title      | Video title only |
| Tags       | Video tags only |
| Channel    | Channel name only |

- Type a keyword and press **Enter** to add it as a filter pill
- Press **Backspace** on an empty field to remove the last pill
- Press **Enter** on an empty field (or click **Search**) to run
- Multiple pills within the same field are OR'd together
- Filters across different fields are AND'd (e.g. Title="minecraft" + Exclude Tags="roblox" both apply)
- Clearing all filters and searching reloads the all-time charts

---

## Results

### Country tabs
After a search, a row of country buttons appears showing only countries that have matching results. Click any to filter the current tab to that country only. "🌍 All" shows results from every country combined.

**Countries covered:** 🇧🇷 Brazil · 🇨🇦 Canada · 🇩🇪 Germany · 🇫🇷 France · 🇬🇧 UK · 🇮🇳 India · 🇯🇵 Japan · 🇰🇷 South Korea · 🇲🇽 Mexico · 🇷🇺 Russia · 🇺🇸 USA

### Result tabs

| Tab | What it shows |
|-----|---------------|
| 🔥 Most Popular | Top 50 by total view count |
| ⚡ Most Controversial | Videos flagged as controversial (see below) |
| 📺 Channels | Channel names that matched your keyword but had no matching videos |
| 🏷️ Top Tags | Most-used tags across all matched videos, clickable to search |

### Video cards
Each video card has four columns:
- **Rank** — position in the list
- **Title + metadata** — click to open a detail popup with full tags and description
- **Stats** — view count, like rate bar, likes and comments
- **Analysis** — tag pills from that video (dominant tags highlighted in blue); the **#1 ranked video** also shows a cross-video summary bar chart (see below)

---

## Analysis widget

The analysis column on each card shows the tags from that video. Tags that appear frequently across the **top 20 results** are highlighted in blue as "dominant."

For the **#1 ranked video**, the widget shows a summary of the top 20:
- **Keyword in Top 20** — what % of the top 20 results contain your primary keyword
- **Co-tags** — the most common tags appearing alongside your keyword across the top 20, shown as percentage bars
- **Channel** — the most dominant channel in the top 20 and how many times it appears
- **Global Tag Popularity** — once loaded, shows what % of the entire 133K dataset has your keyword as a tag, plus the top co-occurring tags globally

Clicking any tag pill runs a new search for that tag.

---

## Controversy criteria

A video is flagged as controversial when it has comments **and** meets at least one of:

- **Like rate < 5%** — fewer than 5 likes per 100 views
- **Comment rate < 0.5%** — fewer than 0.5 comments per 100 views

Videos with zero comments are excluded. The controversial tab ranks by a severity score that weighs both the engagement imbalance and the total view count.

---

## Detail popup

Click any video title to open a popup showing:
- Full title, country, category, channel
- Exact view / like / comment counts and like rate %
- All tags as pills
- Full description text

---

## Requirements

- Python 3.6+
- No extra packages needed (standard library only)

---

## Quick launch (Windows)

To avoid typing commands every time, create a file called `launch.bat` in the same folder with this content:

```
python D:\your-folder\search_server.py
```

Then double-click `launch.bat` to start the server, and open `http://localhost:8765` in your browser.
